    {
      "question": "e-7698",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/problem",
      "old_sha256": "e32371416e181cfb13963147f8293eb928ac7e251acf489252f9ba54c8b0db5d",
      "new_sha256": "bf4cc7949f6adcfcd4ae465eaced14b921e582b303d47c2701e829548c095301",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7698",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/problem",
          "value": "The inverse of the $3 \\times 3$ matrix $K$ is found using row operations, as shown below. Complete the process, and determine $K^{-1}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\text{___________} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 &  &  &  \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 &  &  &  \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 &  &  & \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058199/example-7698.json",
          "file_sha256": "41e780630a4fd7c09c8020bb196137c15b4dae65d93b90d76b5dc7edd8c0477e"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 1874,
      "reason": "Observed MA content supersedes the stored value",
      "action": "replace",
      "transaction_receipt": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058199/edb-import/commit.edn",
      "basis_after": 1875
    },
    {
      "question": "e-7698",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "4e5a0e372e5adf97ffa96e0cef7d6b8df3e06e0c5c3ab94d925a1b0418555dca",
      "new_sha256": "0c9050209739953f6b2a10da45a39cee8f10f3896d10b95a3222cf8faf58e926",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-7698",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "We write down $K$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $K$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & -5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -\\frac{5}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe matrix $K^{-1}$ is given by the right-hand side of our big matrix after all of the transformations have been completed. Therefore, \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058199/assets/cd0988d505bdb5421607608fd469a8b3d738ff64e431037a4b872cc660b9a955.png)",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14058199/example-7698.json",
          "file_sha256": "41e780630a4fd7c09c8020bb196137c15b4dae65d93b90d76b5dc7edd8c0477e"
        }
      ],
      "basis": 1874,
